"Jagged Polynomial Commitments (or: How to Stack Multilinears) (https://eprint.iacr.org/2025/917)\nciteturn7view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn6view1\",\"lineno\":42}); Total lines: 81\nL1: #### Is it made out of cite0†tin foil†iacr.org ?\nL2: \nL3: cite1†Cryptology ePrint Archive L4: \nL5:   * cite2†Papers Updates from the last:\nL6:     * cite3†7 days L7:     * cite4†31 days L8:     * cite5†6 months L9:     * cite6†365 days L10:     * * * *\nL11: \nL12:     * cite7†Listing by year L13:     * cite8†All papers L14:     * cite9†Compact view L15:     * cite10†Subscribe†www.iacr.org L16:     * * * *\nL17: \nL18:     * cite11†How to cite L19:     * * * *\nL20:     * cite12†Harvesting metadata L21:   * cite2†Submissions L22:     * cite13†Submit a paper L23:     * cite14†Revise or withdraw a paper L24:     * cite15†Acceptance and publishing conditions L25: \nL26: cite16†Image: Search Button L27: \nL28: [Input] [Button: Search ]\nL29: #### Paper 2025/917\nL30: \nL31: ### cite17†Jagged Polynomial Commitments (or: How to Stack Multilinears) L32: \nL33: Tamir Hemo, Succinct\nL34: \nL35: Kevin Jue, Succinct\nL36: \nL37: Eugene Rabinovich, Succinct\nL38: \nL39: Gyumin Roh, Succinct\nL40: \nL41: Ron D. Rothblum, Succinct\nL42: ##### Abstract\nL43: Modern SNARK constructions, almost ubiquitously, rely on a polynomial commitment scheme (PCS) --- a method by which a prover can commit to a large polynomial $P$ and later provide evaluation proofs of the form \"P(x)=y\" to the verifier.\nL44: In the context of zkVMs (i.e., proof-systems for general-purpose RAM computations), the common design is to represent the computation trace as a sequence of tables, one per CPU instruction, and commit to the these tables, or even their individual columns, as separate polynomials. Committing separately to these polynomials has a large overhead in verification costs, especially in hash-based systems. In this work we drastically reduce this cost via a new construction which we call the jagged PCS.\nL45: This PCS enables the prover to commit to the entire computation trace as a single polynomial, but then allows for the verifier to emulate access to the individual table or column polynomials, so that the arithmetization can proceed in the usual manner. The jagged PCS may be thought of as a sparse PCS for a very particular form of sparsity - namely, a \"jagged\" matrix in which each column has a different height. Our construction of the jagged PCS is highly performant in practice.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSpeeding Up Sum-Check Proving (Extended Version) (https://eprint.iacr.org/2026/587)\nciteturn7view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn3view2\",\"lineno\":42}); Total lines: 82\nL1: #### Is it made out of cite0†tin foil†iacr.org ?\nL2: \nL3: cite1†Cryptology ePrint Archive L4: \nL5:   * cite2†Papers Updates from the last:\nL6:     * cite3†7 days L7:     * cite4†31 days L8:     * cite5†6 months L9:     * cite6†365 days L10:     * * * *\nL11: \nL12:     * cite7†Listing by year L13:     * cite8†All papers L14:     * cite9†Compact view L15:     * cite10†Subscribe†www.iacr.org L16:     * * * *\nL17: \nL18:     * cite11†How to cite L19:     * * * *\nL20:     * cite12†Harvesting metadata L21:   * cite2†Submissions L22:     * cite13†Submit a paper L23:     * cite14†Revise or withdraw a paper L24:     * cite15†Acceptance and publishing conditions L25: \nL26: cite16†Image: Search Button L27: \nL28: [Input] [Button: Search ]\nL29: #### Paper 2026/587\nL30: \nL31: ### cite17†Speeding Up Sum-Check Proving (Extended Version) L32: \nL33: Quang Dao, Carnegie Mellon University\nL34: \nL35: Zachary DeStefano, New York University\nL36: \nL37: Suyash Bagad, Ingonyama\nL38: \nL39: Yuval Domb, Ingonyama\nL40: \nL41: Justin Thaler, Georgetown University, a16z crypto research\nL42: ##### Abstract\nL43: The sum-check protocol is a foundational primitive in modern cryptographic proof systems, but its prover-side cost has emerged as a concrete bottleneck. This paper introduces three complementary techniques that significantly reduce sum-check proving time and memory, especially in the context of zero-knowledge virtual machines (zkVMs).\nL44: First, for applications involving products of many multilinear polynomials, we develop a new algorithm that significantly reduces the number of field multiplications required for proving. Second, we develop a \"small-value sum-check prover\" algorithm. This significantly speeds up the prover in the common setting where the polynomials being summed evaluate to 64 or 32-bit integers, or to elements of a small sub-field within a larger extension field.\nL45: Even outside of the small-value setting, this algorithm yields a faster \"streaming prover\", by which we mean a small-space algorithm that applies whenever the terms being summed can be enumerated in small space (as arises, for example, in zkVM applications). Third, we nearly eliminate prover overhead in the ubiquitous case where one factor is an equality polynomial by exploiting its decomposable tensor structure.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRelease Release v4.0.0 · ingonyama-zk/icicle · GitHub (https://github.com/ingonyama-zk/icicle/releases/tag/v4.0.0)\nciteturn7view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn3view1\",\"lineno\":155}); Total lines: 269\nL120:   * cite64†Code L121:   * cite65†Issues 27 L122:   * cite66†Pull requests 22 L123:   * cite67†Actions L124:   * cite68†Projects L125:   * cite69†Security and quality 0 L126:   * cite70†Insights L127: \nL128: Additional navigation options\nL129: # Release v4.0.0\nL130: \nL131: cite71†Latest